NLSY79 BA-completion analysis — workflow and findings
======================================================

DATA
----
File: data/nls_raw.csv (12,686 rows × 61 columns).
Headline outcome variable constructed from the residence series:
  dorm_sustained_81_84 = 1 if Wave-of-residence code == 3.0 (DORM/FRATERNITY/SORORITY) is
  observed in ≥2 of the four waves 1981-1984 (and ≥2 of those four waves are observed).
Proxy caveat (one sentence): BA degree completion itself is unobserved in
nls_raw.csv; the analysis uses sustained dormitory residence in 1981-1984 as a
working proxy for BA completion, with documented limitations (commuters,
off-campus housing, post-1984 completions, Greek-life residents who never
completed a BA, etc.). Companion proxy dorm_ever_81_86 is referenced once for
robustness only.

DIAGNOSTICS (required by researcher)
------------------------------------
Outcome prevalence by race (dorm_sustained_81_84, observed portion):
  Black     (race=1): N=1,228; N_yes=26  ; prev=2.12%
  Hispanic  (race=2): N=1,855; N_yes=145 ; prev=7.82%
  NBNH      (race=3): N=5,465; N_yes=410 ; prev=7.50%

ASVAB composite (z-score standardized) by race:
  Black     (race=1): mean=-0.304, sd=0.780, n=2,002
  Hispanic  (race=2): mean=-0.503, sd=0.712, n=3,174
  NBNH      (race=3): mean=+0.292, sd=0.780, n=7,510
  → substantial racial gap in pre-college academic standing.

Refit of Model A as ordinary logit (delta-coefficient interpretability):
  I report ALL coefficients on the log-odds scale. Model A is fitted via
  statsmodels GLM Binomial with logit link (HC1 heteroskedasticity-robust SE).
  Δcoef(A → B) is computed on the log-odds scale with delta-method SE
  Var(β̂_A − β̂_B) = Var(β̂_A) + Var(β̂_B) (independent draws from two
  separate logit fits).

VARIABLES USED
--------------
Demographics:  age_79 (continuous), is_black, is_hispanic (NBNH reference),
               is_female.
SES blocks:
  parents_edu     = mean(mother_grade_1979, father_grade_1979) using listwise
                    rows then per-person mean when at least one parent is
                    observed; an indicator single_parent_missing = 1 if at
                    least one of the two is missing.
  log_income_79   = log( 1 + Total net family income (previous calendar year
                    1979) ). Pre-treatment.
  log_wealth_85   = log( 1 + Family net wealth 1985 ). Concurrent with
                    college-attendance window — partly endogenous. Used ONLY
                    in the sensitivity Model D, NOT in the headline A/B/C trio.
Academic block:
  asvab_composite = per-person mean of the four 1981 ASVAB standardized
                    z-scores (AR/WK/PC/MK), with grand-mean (≈0) fallback
                    for the ~1,500 fully-missing cases (mean-imputed
                    following the binding refinement).
  class_rank_pct  = rank 1981 / class_size 1981, defined only where
                    rank ≤ class size and rank, size > 0; otherwise NaN.
Model C additions: race × ASVAB (black_x_asvab, hispanic_x_asvab) and
                   SES × ASVAB (parents_edu_x_asvab, income79_x_asvab).

MODELS
------
Model A (race + SES + demographics):
  P(dorm_sustained=1) = f(const, is_black, is_hispanic, is_female, age_79,
                            parents_edu, single_parent_missing, log_income_79)
Model B = A + academic block (asvab_composite, class_rank_pct).
Model C = B + (race × ASVAB) + (SES × ASVAB).
Model D (sensitivity, not headline) = C + log_wealth_85 + log_wealth_85 × ASVAB.

ESTIMATOR
---------
Logistic regression (logit link), HC1 heteroskedasticity-robust SE.
All coefficients reported on the LOG-ODDS scale (per binding refinement).
Headline reported quantity: Δcoef Model A → Model B for race indicators
and SES components, computed on the log-odds scale with delta-method SE.
Model C additionally reports AMEs at ASVAB quartiles (Q1, Q2, Q3) by race.

MISSING DATA
------------
Primary: listwise complete-case (n = 2,910 on Model A/B/C; analytic sample
excluded 4,138 observations whose class_rank_pct could not be defined).
Multiple imputation: MICE with m=20, cycles=10, PMM (predictive mean
matching) on continuous, logistic regression on categorical, predictors
included asvab_composite, is_black, is_hispanic, is_female, age_79.
Incarceration auxiliaries (ever_sentenced_pre1980, ever_jailed_through_1996)
are used ONLY inside the imputation model (per binding refinement).
MICE base N = 8,548 (after dropping persons missing on outcome, ASVAB,
sex, age, or race).

CROSS-CHECK CRITERION (per binding refinement)
----------------------------------------------
If listwise and MICE agree on sign and significance of Δcoef(A→B), the
reportable finding is the one they agree on; if they diverge, both are
reported and attrition is discussed as a competing explanation.
Headline Δcoef is stable for parents_edu (both sig positive, both attenuate
the gap) and for is_black (both reductions small, both NOT significant).
Δcoef for is_hispanic is consistent in sign and significance in both.
Diverges on log_income_79 (listwise Δ=+0.02 NS; MICE Δ=+0.13 p=0.15
in the same direction but discussion below).

HEADLINE RESULTS
----------------
Δcoef A → B (log-odds; positive = attenuation toward zero):
                       listwise             MICE
  is_black             −0.144 (p=0.73)      −0.304 (p=0.35)
  is_hispanic          −0.605 (p=0.013)     −0.763 (p<0.001)
  parents_edu          +0.103 (p=0.011)     +0.132 (p<0.001)
  log_income_79        +0.022 (p=0.860)     +0.132 (p=0.153)

INTERPRETATION
--------------
- Adding ASVAB + class-rank ratio halves the parents-education SES coefficient
  (0.166 → 0.064 log-odds; ~62% attenuation in listwise; ~54% in MICE).
- The Black-vs-NBNH gap is NOT significantly attenuated by adding academics
  in either analytic strategy. Residual Black disadvantage ≈ −0.55 log-odds
  in Model B.
- The Hispanic-NBNH contrast *grows more positive* once academics are
  controlled, in both strategies, because Hispanic respondents in the
  analytic sample achieve higher proxy rates than NBNH at the same ASVAB.
- The 1979 log-family-income coefficient is essentially unchanged in listwise
  but moves in the same direction (toward zero) in MICE (p≈0.15). Compromise:
  report both, treat as listwise non-significant but MICE-compatible.
- Race × ASVAB interactions in Model C are not significant at the 5% level
  (black × asvab = −0.67, p=0.10; hispanic × asvab = −0.38, p=0.15). SES ×
  ASVAB interactions are also non-significant (parents_edu × asvab = −0.03,
  p=0.45; income79 × asvab = +0.03, p=0.83).
- AMEs at ASVAB quartiles, Model C: Black BA-proxy rate grows very slowly
  with ASVAB (2.7% at Q1 to 3.5% at Q3); NBNH grows faster (2.7% → 6.9%);
  Hispanic is high throughout (11% → 18%). The Black gap widens with ASVAB.

ROBUSTNESS (Model D, N=2,273): adding log_wealth_85 as a third SES block
  leaves the headline pattern intact: parents_edu Δattenuation remains
  positive (and similar); is_black slightly attenuated (~$+0.05$ towards
  zero); is_hispanic amplified; interactions remain non-significant.

ROBUSTNESS (companion outcome dorm_ever_81_86, N=4,341): when the outcome
  is broadened to enrollment, is_black is strongly attenuated by academics
  (Δcoef = −2.22, p=0.024), in contrast to the precise sustained-dorm
  (BA-proxy) result where Black is not attenuated. This contrast makes
  the proxy-dependence of the conclusion explicit.

STOP RULE
---------
The listwise and MICE results AGREE on the headline Δcoef patterns (parents_edu
significantly attenuated; is_black NOT significantly attenuated; is_hispanic
significantly amplified) so per the binding refinement we STOP without further
sensitivity work.
